# Everything on the field that is numerous, in one sheet.
#
# **34 x 34 texels a frame, at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 20**, which is `2 *
# EnemyKind::Brute.radius()` = 1.7 world units — the largest collider in the
# game, to the texel. Every frame in this file is that size, because a `.crpix`
# declares one frame size for the whole file, and every one of them is *drawn*
# at that size too: a grunt is an 18-texel picture centred in a 34-texel frame,
# so its quad is larger than its body and the difference is transparent.
#
# # Why one sheet and not five
#
# `SpriteRenderer` starts a new batch — a bind and a draw — whenever
# consecutive sprites name a different sheet. Asteroids has three rock sheets
# and has to emit its field largest-first to keep that at three; a field of ten
# thousand walked in spawn order would be ten thousand batches. **With one sheet
# there is no order to get wrong**: the player, all three enemy kinds and the
# pickups are one run however the lists are walked, and `art.rs` needs no
# grouping pass over the crowd at all. That is the property `docs/plan/sample/
# 03-horde.md`'s scale sub-slice measures, so it is the one this file is built
# for.
#
# The price is the transparent margin around the two small kinds — a runner's
# quad is 34 texels wide for 13 texels of art, so about seven times the fill.
# It is bounded by the screen rather than by the horde: enemies settle about
# 1.25 units apart, so a full view holds a few hundred of them and the wasted
# blend is under twice the framebuffer however large the field gets.
#
# # No scale makes every collider a whole number of texels
#
# The radii are 0.45, 0.32 and 0.85 world units, chosen for how the game plays.
# `0.64 * T` is a whole number only for `T` a multiple of 25 and `0.9 * T` only
# for `T` a multiple of 10, so all three boxes land on texels only at `T = 50`
# and up — 85 texels for a brute. Only the *frame* has to be whole, and it is:
# the brute's box at 20 is exactly 34. The other silhouettes are drawn to their
# own collider inside it, to the nearest texel, which
# `art::tests::every_silhouette_is_the_size_of_the_collider_it_stands_for`
# pins against `game.rs`'s radii rather than against numbers repeated here.
#
# # Where 20 comes from
#
# The smallest thing here that has to read as a *shape* is the runner, 0.64
# units across. Three enemy kinds have to be told apart at a glance in a crowd,
# which is a harder ask than "not a circle": each needs a feature — a pair of
# horns, four thin legs, a heavy brow — and a feature needs a texel to stand
# out, a texel to bite back in, and a rim either side. That puts the floor at
# about thirteen texels across, and 13 / 0.64 is 20.3. Half of it would leave
# the runner at six texels, which is a dot. Double it and the brute is 68 rows
# of hand-written art.
#
# The player's box (1.0 unit) then lands on 20 texels and the pickup's (0.7) on
# 14, both exactly.
#
# # The player has a bright rim and everything else has a dark one
#
# Deliberate, and the one place this file breaks its own style. The player is a
# single body in a field of hundreds at the same size; a dark outline on a dark
# ground is what makes it disappear into the crowd, and being unable to find
# yourself is the only unplayable bug a survivors game has.
#
# # The player is a wizard, and the wizard walks
#
# The frames named `player` and `walk-a` … `walk-d` are one figure: a pointed
# hat, a shadowed face with a beard, a robe flaring to a lit hem, and a staff.
# `player` is the standing frame and the rest are the cycle `clip walk` plays.
# Each is drawn to the same collider box as the figure it replaced.
#
# **Two contacts and two passes, rather than one of each, because a robe hides
# the legs.** What reads at this size is the body rising and falling and the hem
# swinging, not a stride — so `walk-a` and `walk-c` are the contacts (the low
# pose, feet apart, the hem bunched on the trailing side) and `walk-b` and
# `walk-d` are the passes (the high pose, one foot planted). The two contacts
# differ in where the feet land and which way the hem is bunched; the two passes
# differ in *which* foot is under the body. A cycle of one contact and one pass
# has to choose between the bob and the step and shows whichever it chose twice
# as often.
#
# **The hold is the `@` on the `clip` line at the bottom of this file, and that
# is the only place it is written.** `Playback` reads it off the baked sheet
# rather than out of `art.rs`, and `art::tests`' `WALK_HOLD_TICKS` is a checksum
# on the round trip through the sidecar rather than a second copy of it. It is
# set so that one cycle carries the wizard a little under one body width per
# step at `game::PLAYER_SPEED` — the feet land about where a stride would put
# them, instead of skating.
#
# **The staff does not move, in any frame.** It is the one part of the figure
# that is nailed down, and that is what makes the muzzle a constant: a bolt
# leaves the head of the staff, and if the head moved with the animation the
# shot would leave a different place on every frame of the cycle. The head is
# the `m` texels — that colour is in the wizard's frames and nowhere else here,
# which is how `art::tests::the_staff_head_is_where_the_muzzle_says_it_is` finds
# it — and their centroid is `game::STAFF_MUZZLE` scaled by
# `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT`, from the centre of the frame. That test measures the
# baked bytes against that constant, so moving the orb in this file without
# moving the constant is a build failure rather than a bolt that misses its own
# staff.
#
# **Drawn facing right, and only facing right.** Facing left is the same frame
# with its `u` range reversed — see `art::mirrored` — so there is no second
# column of art to keep in step with this one.

crpix: 34 34 9 17 1

